---
layout: layouts/base.njk
permalink: /templates/
title: Templates
seo:
  title: "Templates — Andean Road"
  description: "Page templates and map layouts you can pick up and write markdown into — article, itinerary, FAQ, reference, gallery, manual, plus six ways to put a map on the page."
---

{% set crumbs = [{ label: "Templates" }] %}
{% include "partials/breadcrumbs.njk" %}

{% set writing = [] %}
{% set maps = [] %}
{% for t in collections.templates %}
  {% if "map-" in t.fileSlug %}{% set maps = (maps.push(t), maps) %}
  {% else %}{% set writing = (writing.push(t), writing) %}{% endif %}
{% endfor %}

{% set tools = [
  { url: "/tools/color-scales/", path: "pages/tools/color-scales.njk", title: "Color Scales", description: "Blend two brand colors into a 5–11 step scale, preview it in a chart and heatmap, and export as CSS, Figma tokens, SVG, or PNG." },
  { url: "/tools/map-atelier/", path: "tools/map-atelier/editor.html", title: "Map Atelier", description: "A separate app, hosted as-is — pin places on a map, dress it in a palette and pattern, and share it as a self-contained link." },
  { url: "/tools/internet-loom/", path: "pages/tools/internet-loom.njk", title: "Internet Loom", description: "A weaving draft on a grid loom — lift warp and weft threads with heddle dots, start from twill or huckaback, and export the draft as SVG or PNG." },
  { url: "/tools/svg-editor/", path: "tools/svg-editor/index.html", title: "SVG Map Editor", description: "Recolor and manage layers on an embedded SVG map — fill and stroke color groups, per-layer visibility, undo, and export, all in the browser." },
  { url: "/tools/spatial-autocorrelation/", path: "pages/tools/spatial-autocorrelation.njk", title: "Spatial Autocorrelation", description: "A visual reference with a live Gaussian-random-field simulator — drag the correlation slider, watch Moran's I respond, and read LISA cluster maps." },
  { url: "/tools/chromatlas/", path: "pages/tools/chromatlas.njk", title: "Chromatlas", description: "Pantone's Colours of the Year as a browsable atlas, plus a harmony engine that turns any hue into complementary, triadic, and analogous palettes." },
  { url: "/tools/destination-weddings/", path: "pages/tools/destination-weddings.njk", title: "Destination Wedding Websites", description: "A market briefing on destination-wedding software — the numbers, the competitive matrix, and where the jobs-to-be-done scores point." }
] %}

<div class="hero">
  <div class="hero-overline">Write, don't design</div>
  <h1 class="hero-title">Templates</h1>
  <p class="hero-lede">{{ collections.templates | length }} layouts built on the Andean Road tokens — {{ writing | length }} for writing, {{ maps | length }} for maps — plus {{ tools | length }} standalone tool{{ "s" if tools | length != 1 }}. Pick a layout, set it in your front matter, and write markdown. Each page documents its own fields.</p>
</div>

<hr class="rule rule--heavy">

<section class="section">
  <div class="section-label">For writing</div>
  <h2 class="section-title">Page templates</h2>
  <div class="section-description">Every one extends the same base layout and shares one masthead partial, so breadcrumbs, rhythm and dark mode behave identically. Below the masthead each takes a different shape — a timeline, an accordion, a table, a figure grid, a contents rail — chosen to fit how that content is read.</div>

  {% for t in writing %}
  <a href="{{ t.url }}" class="guide-entry">
    <div class="guide-entry__overline">layouts/{{ t.fileSlug }}.njk</div>
    <h3 class="guide-entry__title">{{ t.data.title }}</h3>
    <div class="guide-entry__description">{{ t.data.summary or t.data.subtitle }}</div>
  </a>
  {% endfor %}
</section>

<section class="section">
  <div class="section-label">For maps</div>
  <h2 class="section-title">Map layouts</h2>
  <div class="section-description">Six ways to put a map on a page, each answering a different question. They share one engine and one set of cartographic tokens — the categorical palette, the marker shapes, the legend — so a reader moving between them is always reading the same map language.</div>

  {% for t in maps %}
  <a href="{{ t.url }}" class="guide-entry">
    <div class="guide-entry__overline">layouts/{{ t.fileSlug }}.njk</div>
    <h3 class="guide-entry__title">{{ t.data.title }}</h3>
    <div class="guide-entry__description">{{ t.data.summary or t.data.subtitle }}</div>
  </a>
  {% endfor %}
</section>

<section class="section">
  <div class="section-label">Tools</div>
  <h2 class="section-title">Utilities</h2>
  <div class="section-description">Not templates — standalone pages and apps that live outside a markdown file. Some share the Andean Road tokens, some bring their own.</div>

  {% for t in tools %}
  <a href="{{ t.url }}" class="guide-entry">
    <div class="guide-entry__overline">{{ t.path }}</div>
    <h3 class="guide-entry__title">{{ t.title }}</h3>
    <div class="guide-entry__description">{{ t.description }}</div>
  </a>
  {% endfor %}
</section>

<hr class="rule rule--section">

<section class="section">
  <div class="section-label">Getting started</div>
  <h2 class="section-title">Adding a page</h2>

  <div class="callout">
    <div class="callout__label">The whole contract</div>
    <p>Create a markdown file, set <code>layout: layouts/&lt;name&gt;.njk</code> and a <code>title</code>. Everything else in the front matter is optional and degrades cleanly when left out. Map layouts additionally take a list of place slugs — the coordinates come from the place files, so a map never holds its own copy of the data.</p>
  </div>
</section>
